" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155913 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155913 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155913 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186544481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155913 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "676f7b35-5434-55eb-ad55-37dab460907b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157099 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157099 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3b/3b6739458c31d4ce752549f42083a66dc7a0a0914ac210837d0a041019d914c3.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sin\\left( 2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right)$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\pi/8}^{3\\pi/8} \\sin^2\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind an integral that represents the total area bounded by the polar curve." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157099 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98bc19b4-ab9d-51e3-9190-965bdc787845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157099 #edb/ref 1074 "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2 \\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\pi/8}^{3\\pi/8} \\sin^2\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\nA_{\\textrm{total}}=4\\cdot A = 2 \\int_{-\\pi/8}^{3\\pi/8} \\sin^2\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157100 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157100 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the total area bounded by the polar curve $r=\\sin(7\\theta),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d9aa4ff23010048536fbcbadc9cff5dc2fc094895c00814dd9efccdc9750682a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157100 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5caa3fbc-f232-5e40-b6d2-c34fa552d6b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157100 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta$ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sin(7\\theta) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $7\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n7\\theta = 0, \\pi \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{7}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{7}.$\n\n![](images/71/712d56708d2346f502a229c20596e7aa24ce4be13564e9461784c9114c55065a.png)\n\nSince there are $7$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =7\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14\\theta ))\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\frac{7}{4}(\\theta -\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14\\theta ))|}_{0}^{\\pi /7} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{7}-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))-(0-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{7}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157101 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7368" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157101 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e8/e89c77448446dccb3ececd6f05f1a2e410f18f5077e50c88144cd2101cec9d54.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\cos\\left(3\\theta-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA = 2 \\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi/12}^{\\pi/4} \\cos^2\\left(3\\theta-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nBy changing the limits of integration, find an integral expression representing the total area bounded by the polar curve." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157101 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a994968f-db5b-57f7-b836-f3ec295bfd63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157101 #edb/ref 1074 "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA = 2 \\int_{-\\pi/12}^{\\pi/4} \\cos^2\\left(3\\theta-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ to $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ and the length of this interval is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} - \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n![](images/b9/b98a92acf42831b84597662f03865f0c5bdb831af0573206c56f33ee6f3528ae.svg)\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ to $\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nA_\\textrm{total} = 2 \\int_{-\\pi/12}^{11\\pi/12} \\cos^2\\left(3\\theta-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157295 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157295 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157295 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157295 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157295 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157295 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157295 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Integral Expression For the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157295 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157295 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ce9c0740-2003-5c99-84f2-294c88d57ba7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157296 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157296 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157296 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157296 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157296 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "09043c6b-599d-5c5d-ac4b-20dd6275a8f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157297 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157297 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157297 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157297 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157297 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157297 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157297 #edb/ref 1039 "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve By Varying the Limits of Integration" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157297 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157297 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e93116e7-55c4-5685-8208-a35c627117a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157489 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157489 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157489 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157489 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157489 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157489 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157489 #edb/ref 1045 "The Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157489 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157489 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157489 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157489 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "924e2aff-70c8-5662-9585-30e32759e61f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157828 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157828 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157828 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157828 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cd/cd3af53e4d4f02c5978e8b4bc8508fb0455a0a09e7222377df68a2297bc30dae.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157828 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34c5bc2e-d431-58fd-be86-bcbb846410a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157828 #edb/ref 1074 "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=4\\cdot A=8{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157829 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157829 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157829 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/47/47eb2f1073f62129ba2fbf7ae154b9733ff2d5bf09af30053f7b708d7a8d21b0.png)\n\nFind the total area bounded by the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "527ee65c-de13-51b9-8ea3-2fa79ecc1df6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157829 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n2\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/22/220bd5e2559f7e3ef483b1315d1dd98771cf81d8d181a161b87d8084773259e6.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =4\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={(\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157831 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157831 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157831 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157831 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/09/09bc06001905956e32a4ff8cfff25a875a6933e19c365b2f9b159d8711a05048.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157831 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b62e1087-3b4a-589d-abc5-58ac8a8c24f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157831 #edb/ref 1074 "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/09/09bc06001905956e32a4ff8cfff25a875a6933e19c365b2f9b159d8711a05048.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=3\\cdot A=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157832 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157832 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83527" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157832 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157832 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3a/3aa82e021a1871bd17abfd72570452bc6aefe358a8fddd3cb8e92bb6ff91c5a9.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157832 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7ae6625-2462-5c24-ab37-c40b68057a97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157832 #edb/ref 1074 "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/3a/3aa82e021a1871bd17abfd72570452bc6aefe358a8fddd3cb8e92bb6ff91c5a9.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $4$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,{{[\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}]}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{11\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158136 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158136 #edb/ref 1061 27331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158136 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46bff2e6-5534-59d5-8395-e50507c10b20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158136 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158137 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158137 #edb/ref 1061 27332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158137 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6274289a-17a6-5a8c-aebd-822328b86ef8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158137 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158138 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158138 #edb/ref 1061 27333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158138 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c2f89fc1-eb29-50ee-92a1-d9365468ebdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158138 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158139 #edb/ref 1061 27334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158139 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34f635d8-7098-582d-a722-326a66beefae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158139 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158140 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158140 #edb/ref 1061 27417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158140 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "386f572e-8a18-54a0-b592-6c5f0e677c8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158140 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158484 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/34/34fdc5325fe4b1fb73d203ccb9db81e5d8b4b1cb02b3efda2b928addc2e458e6.png)\n\nSuppose that the area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sin(5\\theta)$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the **total area** bounded by the curve? \n\nOne method is to count the number of petals and multiply the area of one petal by the total number of petals. In our case, we have $5$ petals in total, and so the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\nA_\\textrm{total}=5A =  \\dfrac{5}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158484 #edb/ref 1065 4441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158484 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2ceb97ae-7dd4-5b54-b6f0-ccfa1ff9c1c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158484 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158485 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/34/34fdc5325fe4b1fb73d203ccb9db81e5d8b4b1cb02b3efda2b928addc2e458e6.png)\n\nLet's go back to our polar curve $r=\\sin(5\\theta).$ Recall that the area bounded by one petal is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nCan we find an expression for the **total area** bounded by the curve by varying the limits of integration only?\n\nIndeed, we can, and we do this by extending the upper bound of the integral to include all $5$ loops. \n\nNote that:\n\n- The $1$st petal is traversed from $\\theta_1=0$ to $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{5},$ and the length of this interval is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}.$\n\n- To find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{5} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{2\\pi }{5} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nContinuing this method, we obtain the following intervals for all $5$ petals of our rose:\n\n![](images/81/814d2ff9a4ca7e8c7e6d85cc374eff37034e541338705c2abf621e5fd82f90e0.png)\n\n![](images/6c/6cc39d6b6fdf29c3b582f867ea9e769b5c6bf466bc57d53a3f346e092375b89c.svg)\n\nNote that the curve traces the petals out of order. However, all $5$ petals are traced out as $\\theta$ ranges from $0$ to $\\pi.$\n\nTherefore, we can obtain the total area of the given polar curve if we traverse it from $\\theta = 0$ to $\\theta = \\pi \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nA_\\textrm{total} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It might be tempting to extend the top bound to $2\\pi.$ However, the entire curve is traced out on the interval $\\theta\\in [0,\\pi],$ so integrating from $0$ to $2\\pi$ would give us *twice* the total area bounded by the curve!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158485 #edb/ref 1065 4455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158485 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "be01da54-00a0-5ce1-8112-30c4e3bd1ded" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158485 #edb/ref 1067 "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single